clearcote: a Windows persona on a non-D3D host must not advertise ASTC/ETC.

D3D11 cannot expose WEBGL_compressed_texture_astc, _etc or _etc1 at all, so their presence beside a
renderer string naming Direct3D11 is a self-contained contradiction. Measured: the container
advertised all three; genuine Chrome 151 on real D3D11 exposes none.

Hooked at ExtensionSupportedAndAllowed(), the SINGLE gate feeding both getSupportedExtensions() and
getExtension(), so the advertised set and the gettable set cannot drift apart -- an
enumerate-then-get mismatch is a one-line detector and no real browser produces a null there.

The extension objects are consequently never constructed, and AddCompressedTextureFormat() is only
reachable from those constructors, so getParameter(COMPRESSED_TEXTURE_FORMATS) stops listing their
enums without a second, separately-maintained filter. Verified on all three surfaces at once,
decoding the enum ranges (ASTC 0x93b0-0x93dd, ETC2/EAC 0x9270-0x9279, ETC1 0x8d64) rather than
trusting the chain: advertised [], gettable [], banned enums [].

Subtractive only -- it removes a claim and never adds one, so unlike an upward spoof it can never
advertise capability the host cannot back.

KNOWN RESIDUAL: this leaves 27 extensions where genuine D3D11 reports 32. The five-extension gap is
a pre-existing under-claim (the software backend lacks entries a real GPU has), not something this
patch introduces -- it removes 3 impossible ones from 30. Under-claiming is a coherent smaller list;
over-claiming is an incoherent larger one. Closing the remaining gap is separate work.

--- a/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgl/webgl_rendering_context_base.cc
+++ b/third_party/blink/renderer/modules/webgl/webgl_rendering_context_base.cc
@@ -4089,8 +4089,38 @@
   return false;
 }
 
+// clearcote(r16): extensions a Direct3D11 backend cannot expose, blocked when the persona claims
+// Windows on a non-D3D host. ANGLE's Vulkan/SwiftShader backend advertises ASTC and ETC because
+// the Linux driver has them; a real D3D11 device does not, so they sit beside a renderer string
+// naming Direct3D11 and contradict it. Measured against genuine Chrome 151 on real D3D11: none of
+// the three present, 32 extensions total.
+//
+// This is the single gate used by BOTH getSupportedExtensions() and getExtension(), so the
+// advertised set and the gettable set stay the same set -- an enumerate-then-get mismatch is a
+// one-line detector. The extension objects are never constructed as a result, and
+// AddCompressedTextureFormat() is only reachable from those constructors, so
+// getParameter(COMPRESSED_TEXTURE_FORMATS) stops listing their enums without a second filter.
+//
+// Subtractive: it removes a claim and never adds one, so unlike an upward limit spoof it can never
+// advertise capability the host cannot back.
+static bool CcPersonaBlocksExtension(const String& name) {
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_LINUX)
+  if (ungoogled::CurrentPersona().webgl_real ||
+      ungoogled::GetPersonaPlatform() != ungoogled::PersonaPlatform::kWindows) {
+    return false;
+  }
+  return name == "WEBGL_compressed_texture_astc" ||
+         name == "WEBGL_compressed_texture_etc" ||
+         name == "WEBGL_compressed_texture_etc1";
+#else
+  return false;
+#endif
+}
+
 bool WebGLRenderingContextBase::ExtensionSupportedAndAllowed(
     const ExtensionTracker* tracker) {
+  if (CcPersonaBlocksExtension(String(tracker->ExtensionName())))
+    return false;
   if (tracker->Draft() &&
       !RuntimeEnabledFeatures::WebGLDraftExtensionsEnabled())
     return false;
